  "question": {
    "text": "Electrons in a certain energy level <math> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo> <mo>=</mo> <msub> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo> </mrow> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>, can emit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> spectral lines. When they are in another energy level, <math> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo> <mo>=</mo> <msub> <mrow> <mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo> </mrow> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>, they can emit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math> spectral lines. The ratio of orbital speeds of the electrons is",
